echnique found throughout laboratories  but it's especially found in analytical  laboratories  in general the hplc procedure involves  pumps moving solvents  containing  a mixture of compounds through columns  which is known as the stationary phase  that is generally comprised of absorbent  materials  when those components actually hit that  stationary phase or the absorbent  material they will interact differently  causing them to separate into their  individual components  then we can later identify those  this is  commonly found  in research settings medical settings  and manufacturing settings  moving on to ultra high performance  liquid chromatography or uh plc  it's really a very similar method to  liquid chromatography  uh and it's still trying to separate  different compounds found in a mixture  um  however it's really kind of just usually  utilizing a higher pressure to  pull apart those compounds in that  mixture again this is going to be  commonly found in a pharmaceutical  medical and research setting  moving on to supercritical fluid  chromatography or  or s  fc  um  again we're still  using principles similar to high  performance liquid chromatography uh  it's just the main difference here is  that  supercritical fluid chromatography uses  a supercritical fluid such as carbon  dioxide as the mobile phase  this is used in the analysis and  purification of specifically of low to  moderate weight  low to moderate molecular weight  compounds  those that are  thermally liable and chiral compounds as  well  this is a less  used technique but it's definitely used  in  a lot in the pharmaceutical sector  um moving on and i i know we've just  touched on it on a few uh chromo  chromatography techniques but uh  definitely wanted to sit here and hit on  um the liquid chromatography and mass  spectrometry uh method so obviously with  the liquid chromatography we're  separating out compounds  in a mixture  again using the mobile phase and  stationary phase interaction to pull  those  those mixtures of or those compounds out  of the mixture so we can identify them  but in  liquid chromatography mass spectrometry  we're actually  then pairing that with mass analysis as  well  this is used to analyze a variety of  compounds  biochemical organic or inorganic  compounds and this can commonly be found  and is used in biotechnology  the environmental  sector food and agriculture and  cosmetics as well  uh another um  sensitive analytical technique that is  found in a variety of laboratories is  going to be your polymerized change re  chain reaction um  techniques  this technique  is really designed to amplify and target  a specific sequence of dna and rna  there are  the two main ones are going to be  real-time pcr and then quantitative  methods which is also known as endpoint  pcr detection  this is used to analyze following the  following compounds it could be  pathogens so it could be a bacterial  pathogen such as  e coli or salmonella species  it could be a virus it could be a  hepatitis virus or a norovirus and a  common this is this technique is going  to be commonly found in biotechnology  the medical field  specific specifically the clinical lab  the food industry and also found in  research settings  now that we have identified some  uh pretty sensitive analytical  techniques i think it's  definitely worth our time to just  identify and be aware of common  contaminants that are found throughout  the laboratory  some of the residual contaminants that  we often see are solvents um these  solvents can be uh  really anything mostly organic inorganic  or polar  we also see a lot of residual  contaminants being acids and bases  there are a lot of  recommended glassware washing procedures  where the glassware actually goes  through a  acid pre-rinse to really kind of loosen  up stubborn contaminants so it's always  important to properly wash glassware  because if that's the case  that this doesn't happen  we can introduce  residual